{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Franken FDK AAC \u8212- a laboratory/"geek" AAC encoder (FDK)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Built on top of {\b libfdk-aac 2.0.3} (mstorsjo/fdk-aac) + the {\b nu774/fdkaac 1.0.2} frontend, with bolted-on CLI switches that expose the normally hardcoded, internal decisions of the FDK encoder. It serves extreme debugging and experimentation with AAC/HE-AAC/HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Binaries (static, no external DLLs):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe} \u8212- Windows 64-bit (PE32+)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 fdkaac-franken-x86.exe} \u8212- Windows 32-bit (PE32)\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Download\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Prebuilt Windows binaries are published as GitHub Releases, built and hosted by GitHub (no login needed to download):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b \u8594 ? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/michaldziwisz/franken-fdk-aac/releases/latest"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
https://github.com/michaldziwisz/franken-fdk-aac/releases/latest
}}}
}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Grab {\f1 franken-fdk-aac-x64-vX.Y.Z.zip} (64-bit) or the {\f1 x86} one; each zip holds the {\f1 .exe} plus the full documentation. There are no binaries committed to the repo \u8212- see "Build" at the bottom for building it yourself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All original nu774 frontend options ({\f1 -p/--profile}, {\f1 -b/--bitrate}, {\f1 -m/--bitrate-mode}, {\f1 -w/--bandwidth}, {\f1 -a/--afterburner}, {\f1 -s/--sbr-ratio}, {\f1 -f/--transport-format}, tagging, etc.) work as before. Below are only the NEW switches. See also {\f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe --help}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 NOTE: this is an "I-know-what-I'm-doing" tool. Most of these parameters deliberately let you go beyond what the FDK automation does \u8212- you can knowingly wreck the stereo image, the bandwidth, or the quality with them. The sentinel {\f1 -1} (or {\f1 0} for {\f1 --core-cutoff}) = "leave FDK's default behavior".\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Author: Micha\u322 ? Dziwisz. Subject-matter consultant: Patryk Faliszewski. Built on open source: libfdk-aac (Fraunhofer IIS) + the nu774/fdkaac frontend.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Option groups overview (grouped the same way in {\f1 --help})\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The options are ordered by topic, roughly from easiest to most geeky. The same order applies in {\f1 --help} (groups A\u8211-E) and in the sections below:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b A. Start here (consumer):} {\f1 --verbose}, {\f1 --is-aggression}, {\f1 --speech}, {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth}, {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} \u8212- sections 0, 1, 2, 10.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b B. Stereo:} MS ({\f1 --msmask}, {\f1 --msbands}, {\f1 --msbands-lo/-hi}, {\f1 --ms-bias}, {\f1 --ms-precision}), IS ({\f1 --is}, {\f1 --isbands}, {\f1 --is-*}), PS ({\f1 --ps}, {\f1 --ps-iid-quant}, {\f1 --ps-icc}, {\f1 --ps-icc-mode}) \u8212- sections 1, 4, 8.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b C. Bandwidth and SBR:} {\f1 --core-cutoff}, {\f1 --sbr-*} \u8212- sections 2, 3.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b D. Masking / noise / detail:} {\f1 --ath-scale}, {\f1 --spread-mask}, {\f1 --tns-*}, {\f1 --pns}, {\f1 --pns-start}, {\f1 --force-pns} \u8212- sections 5, 6.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b E. Blocks and bitrate:} {\f1 --block-bias}, {\f1 --vbr-reservoir}, {\f1 --peak-bitrate}, {\f1 --max-bits-frame}, {\f1 --min-bits-frame}, {\f1 --bitres-mode} \u8212- sections 7, 9.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tip: at the end, {\f1 --verbose} prints a "franken overrides applied" section \u8212- exactly those switches which, in a given run, deviate from pure FDK.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 0. Diagnostics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --verbose}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before encoding, it dumps to stderr the REAL parameters chosen by the encoder (not just your overrides): AOT, bitrate/mode, samplerate, channel-mode, the EFFECTIVE core cutoff in Hz, afterburner, transport, signaling, plus the state of the coding tools chosen by the encoder (TNS on/off, PNS on/off, Intensity stereo on/off, MS stereo on/off). When SBR is active: sbr-ratio + effective start/stop freq index, freq scale, noise bands, amp res. At the end, a list of your overrides (-1/0 = not set, left to the encoder). Perfect for learning the starting point (e.g. the default HE-AAC v2 48k cutoff = 8613 Hz).\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 1. Joint stereo \u8212- MS / IS / independent stereo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By default FDK itself decides per-band about MS (mid/side) and IS (intensity). Here you can override these decisions and force extreme configurations.\par}
