{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intensity stereo in practice (how to use it, not the formulas)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What it is: intensity stereo (IS) in the upper bands drops separate L/R and sends ONE energy envelope + direction (panning) information. The ear localizes high tones poorly, so this saves quite a few bits \u8212- but at the cost of stereo separation (the width of the scene at the top of the band narrows). You pay for it especially on material with a real L/R difference in the highs (cymbals on one side, spatial effects).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By default FDK is very CAUTIOUS with IS (hence your observation "you can barely hear the difference"). There are three reasons, and that is what these knobs are for:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab IS admission gate: FDK considers IS at all only when {\f1 bitrate/band < 5}. At higher bitrates IS is disallowed entirely. {\f1 --is-aggression >=1} removes this gate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Correlation threshold ({\f1 --is-corr-thresh}, Q8, 256=1.0, default 243 ~= 0.95): both channels must be at least ~95% similar to each other in a given band for IS to turn on. That is very high. Lower it -> IS catches more often, even when the channels are less similar. E.g. 180 (~0.70) = much more aggressive. Too low = audible direction errors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Min. region length ({\f1 --is-min-sfbs}, default 6): IS turns on only on a band of at least 6 consecutive SFBs. Lower it to 1-2 -> IS also catches short fragments.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relationship between them: for a given SFB to go into IS, ALL conditions MUST be met at once \u8212- the admission gate AND correlation above the threshold AND a sufficiently long region AND a stable direction. That is why lowering just one threshold often does nothing (another still blocks it) \u8212- and that is why you usually see no difference by manipulating correlation alone. {\f1 --is-aggression} moves ALL of them at once, coherently.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How to set it:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Simplest: {\f1 --is 1 --is-aggression 40} and listen. Too little IS -> raise to 70, 100. Too much (the scene "glues together" at the top, direction artifacts) -> go down.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab 0 = FDK default (practically IS is barely active at typical bitrates).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab 100 = maximum: gate removed, correlation loose (~0.475), region from 1 SFB, wide direction tolerance. Many bands in IS, strongly audible, saves bits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Manual tuning only when you want precision: set {\f1 --is-aggression 0} and turn {\f1 --is-corr-thresh} (the main one), then {\f1 --is-min-sfbs}, finally {\f1 --is-lr-ratio}. The --is-* values OVERRIDE what the aggression slider set.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Diagnostics: {\f1 --verbose} shows the effective thresholds (IS corr threshold Q8, min SFBs), so you see what actually went into the encoder.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MS/IS bias (point 2): the above {\f1 --is-*} control WHEN the encoder chooses intensity stereo (decision thresholds from the FDK tuning table), independently of the hard on/off. {\f1 --msbands} limits MS to the lower bands (correctly \u8212- the mask and the L/R->M/S butterfly are synchronized, no "left=center, right=rest" artifact).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Completely independent stereo: {\f1 --msmask 0 --is 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab "Laboratory" restriction of MS to the lower bands: e.g. {\f1 --msbands 6}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Forced full MS: {\f1 --msmask 1}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab More eager IS: lower {\f1 --is-corr-thresh} (e.g. 150) and/or {\f1 --is-min-sfbs}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 MS band range: --msbands, --msbands-lo, --msbands-hi (IMPORTANT, often confused)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The spectrum bands are numbered FROM THE BOTTOM: band 0 = lowest frequencies (bass), the higher the number, the higher in the spectrum. In a typical LC stereo there are about 49 of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are TWO independent ways to limit where MS is applied:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\f1 --msbands <n>} \u8212- "the lower N bands". MS allowed ONLY in bands 0..(n-1), i.e. from the bass upward to number n. This is always counted FROM THE BOTTOM. Example: {\f1 --msbands 6} = MS only on the 6 lowest bands, the rest pure L/R.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\f1 --msbands-lo <lo>} + {\f1 --msbands-hi <hi>} \u8212- "FROM-TO range". MS allowed ONLY in bands numbered from {\f1 lo} to {\f1 hi} inclusive. Outside that range, pure L/R. It is a pair \u8212- you provide both. It lets you place MS ANYWHERE, including at the very top.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A concrete example (assuming ~49 bands in LC):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab You want MS ONLY on the 5 HIGHEST bands (e.g. to merge noise at the top and leave the bottom in full independent stereo)? The highest bands are numbers 44..48: {\f1 --msbands-lo 44 --msbands-hi 48}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab You want MS only in the MIDDLE of the band (e.g. 10..30)? {\f1 --msbands-lo 10 --msbands-hi 30}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab You want MS on the 6 LOWEST? Simpler {\f1 --msbands 6} (or {\f1 --msbands-lo 0 --msbands-hi 5} \u8212- the same).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mnemonic: {\f1 --msbands} = "from the bottom up to", {\f1 --msbands-lo/-hi} = "from..to". How many bands you actually have for a given mode/samplerate is shown by {\f1 --verbose} (the "active SFBs" field).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2. Cutoff of the AAC core when SBR is active\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The standard {\f1 -w/--bandwidth} in FDK is IGNORED when SBR is active (HE-AAC v1/v2) \u8212- because {\f1 sbrEncoder_Init()} overrides the bandwidth with a value from the SBR table.\par}
{
