\cellx2160\cellx4320\cellx6480\cellx8640
\trkeep\intbl
{
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ms-bias <n>}\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 0..255 (Q8)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 -1 (off)\par}
\cell}
{{\pard\intbl \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Shifts the L/R vs MS decision threshold. Q8, 128 = +0.5 in FDK's ld64 units. >0 = MS more eager. Reacts already from ~32 (after scale recalibration).\par}
\cell}
}
\intbl\row}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HONESTLY about {\f1 --ms-bias} \u8212- this is the weakest tool in the whole set, and now we know why "it does little". MS (mid/side) is a LOSSLESS transform: mid=L+R, side=L-R reconstructs exactly back to L/R. Enabling/disabling MS on a given band does NOT change the sound \u8212- it only changes HOW MANY BITS the encoding takes. The encoder chooses a near-optimal decision per band anyway; {\f1 --ms-bias} only shifts a few BORDERLINE bands. Measurement (L/R correlation after decoding, ADTS size): effect on the order of <0.1% of the size and correlation changes in the 4th decimal place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Technical note: in the previous version the bias scale was ~256x too weak (multiplier <<15 instead of <<23) \u8212- hence "128 did nothing, only 2048 moved something". Now 128 = a real +0.5 ld64 as in the description, so it reacts from ~32. But even a correctly scaled bias inherently has a small impact (see above).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Want to REALLY control MS? Use the hard switches, not the bias:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --msmask 0} \u8212- DISABLE MS entirely (pure, independent L/R). This is the right choice for center-cancel / vocal removal (zero channel mixing by the coder).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --msmask 1} \u8212- force MS on all bands (max bit economy).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --msbands} / {\f1 --msbands-lo/-hi} \u8212- restrict MS to selected bands. Measurement: msmask 0 vs 1 gives ~900 B difference on a 2s sample; ms-bias only ~2 B.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 9. Quasi-constrained VBR (CBR engine + wider breathing)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 IMPORTANT: without these switches, CBR is 100% UNCHANGED (verified: bit-identical to the original binary). You enable them deliberately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How AAC breathes: even in CBR, frames borrow from the bit-reservoir, so one frame can be ~122 kbps and the next ~141, as long as the average = target. These knobs widen/narrow that breathing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HARD CEILING for everything: an AAC frame holds MAX 6144 bits per channel (=768 bytes/channel); stereo => 12288 bits/frame. At 44100 Hz one frame = 1024 samples = ~23.2 ms, so bits/frame = kbps * 23.22. (E.g. 128k stereo: average ~2972 bits/frame; ceiling 12288.)\par}
{
