{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Examples\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # Starting point \u8212- what the encoder set:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 48000 --verbose -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Quasi-constrained VBR ~128k (safe preset):\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 160000 --vbr-reservoir 6000 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Aggressive intensity stereo at 64k:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 64000 --is 1 --is-aggression 70 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Force PNS at 24k (otherwise the FDK gate disables it):\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 24000 --pns 1 --force-pns -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Audiophile full 40 kHz band from a 96 kHz input:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 320000 --uncap-bandwidth --core-cutoff 40000 -o out.m4a in96k.wav\line
\line
# MS on the 5 highest bands + shallower holes (not the lowest):\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --msbands-lo 44 --msbands-hi 48 --ms-precision 448 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Extremely low HE-AAC v2: 8000 bps stereo 48 kHz:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 8000 --unlock-bitrate -o out.m4a in48k.wav\line
\line
# SBR: full stereo separation LR + forced inverse filtering:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 5 -b 96000 --sbr-stereo-mode 1 --sbr-invf 2 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Your case: 7.5 kHz of core under HE-AAC v2 48k:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 48000 --core-cutoff 7500 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Completely independent stereo (no MS/IS) on LC 128k:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --msmask 0 --is 0 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# MS only on the 6 lower bands:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 96000 --msmask 1 --msbands 6 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Only short blocks + limited TNS:\line
# Only long blocks (bias) + limited TNS:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 96000 --block-bias 0 --tns-order 2 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# More aggressive masking (thresholds x2) + earlier PNS:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 80000 --ath-scale 512 --pns 1 --pns-start 4000 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Denser SBR noise description + more precise amplitude:\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-noise-bands 5 --sbr-amp-res 0 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# HE-AAC v2 with PS disabled (flattened stereo):\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 32000 --ps 0 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Default quality vs the original binary (fdkaac2.exe)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Verification (2026-07-21) whether franken's default settings = the original supplied binary (dBpoweramp R17, the same libfdk-aac version 4.0.1 / package 2.0.3):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab AAC-LC: {\b bit-identical} (the same md5) \u8212- zero regressions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab HE-AAC v1/v2: the bytes differ, BUT: the HE-AAC v2 spectrum is identical to 0.000 dB, and the HE-AAC v1 error relative to the original is practically the same as in the original (7.05e11 vs 7.05e11). The byte difference stems from a different compiler (mingw/gcc vs MSVC) and fixed-point rounding, NOT from worse quality. The audible SBR "otherness" is a different, equally correct implementation, not a quality loss.\sa180\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Tests (make check)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Upstream fdk-aac/nu774 have no unit tests ({\f1 make check} in them = a no-op). This project has its OWN functional test suite in {\f1 tests/check.sh}, run with:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 make check\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It checks (on the x64 + x86 binaries if present): completeness of the switches in --help, decodability of the stream for each switch (ffmpeg, 0 errors), real operation of quasi-CVBR (CVBR breathes wider than the rigid bitres-mode2), verbose without -1 values, and NO REGRESSION (default CBR without franken flags = ADTS bit-identical to the original fdkaac2.exe). Requires ffmpeg + python3 (present in WSL). Exit 0 = OK, 1 = errors, 77 = missing dependencies. Last result: 27/27 PASS.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The sources with the applied patches live in {\f1 src-fdk-aac/} and {\f1 src-fdkaac/}. All changes in libfdk are wired through a single module {\f1 libAACenc/src/franken.\{h,cpp\}} (the global {\f1 g_franken} block, sentinels = FDK defaults), and the new {\f1 AACENC_PARAM}s (range {\f1 0xF0xx}) are exposed in {\f1 aacenc_lib.h}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 sudo apt-get install -y mingw-w64          # + autotools (autoconf/automake/libtool)\line
\line
# --- libfdk-aac (x64) ---\line
cd src-fdk-aac && autoreconf -i\line
./configure --host=x86_64-w64-mingw32 --prefix=$PWD/../inst-x64 \\\line
    --enable-static --disable-shared CFLAGS=-O2 CXXFLAGS=-O2\line
make -j && make install\line
\line
# --- frontend (x64) ---\line
cd ../src-fdkaac && autoreconf -i\line
PKG_CONFIG_PATH=$PWD/../inst-x64/lib/pkgconfig ./configure \\\line
    --host=x86_64-w64-mingw32 CFLAGS="-O2 -I$PWD/../inst-x64/include" \\\line
    LDFLAGS="-static -static-libgcc -L$PWD/../inst-x64/lib"\line
make -j     # -> fdkaac.exe\line
\line
# x86: the same with --host=i686-w64-mingw32 and a separate inst-x86 prefix.\par}
